Falsifies `system_tests::user::receive_cap_attack_tests::a_send_cap_to_a_plain_receive_installs_nothing_whichever_side_parks_first`.

Architecture: aarch64

The defect is the tree as it stood until §246 (a plain `RECEIVE` never takes a capability),
PROVISIONAL number, calef's ruling A of 2026-10-04 UTC. The patch restores `ipc_send_cap`'s
receiver-first arm as it was: it installs the delegation in whatever receiver is parked, without
asking whether that receiver is in `RECEIVE_CAP`. A plain `RECEIVE` parked first then ends holding
one more slot than it held, and `x1` is that slot, where the sender-first order returns `0`.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(got.held_after, got.held_before, "a plain RECEIVE ended holding 1
slots, having held 0 (receiver_first = true): the delegation was installed in a table that never
asked for it")`. Replayed red on aarch64 on 2026-10-04 (UTC) against the tree before the fix, with
this assertion. Not replayed on riscv64 or x86_64 here; CI's re-falsify job covers them.

**The vacuity guards stay green under the patch.** The receiver parks (`rendezvous_waiting_receivers
== 1`) before the sender runs, so the order is the one claimed, and `words[0] == DATA` says the
delegation's data word really arrived at the plain `RECEIVE`.

Nothing here is architecture-specific: `ipc_send_cap` is portable scheduler code. The sender-first
half it leaves alone is milestone 633 (an outside agent attacks the confinement claim)'s.

Regenerated 2026-10-04 (UTC) by the lane for the proposal "capability lookup off the global lock",
which moved each thread's capability table behind its own lock: the delivery now goes through
`deliver_capability`, so the old hunk no longer matched. Same removals, same defect.
Replayed red on aarch64 the same day, at the receiver-first assertion this record predicts.
Regenerated 2026-10-07 (UTC) by the lane for milestone 779 (fuzz the surface a confined process can
reach), whose capability-spelling pass
renamed the bare `cap` locals in `ipc_send_cap_from` to `capability` (the cap-abbreviation
ratchet, calef 2026-10-06): same removals, same defect, new local names.

Re-cut 2026-10-08 (UTC) by milestone 139 (drive the unsafe count down) round 10's lane, the
scheduler queue ownership token: `rendezvous.send` now takes the sender's queue token and hands
back the receiver's, so the hunk's context grew `hold_token(me)` and `hold_token(receiver)`. Same
removals, same defect, same local names.

Regenerated 2026-10-11 (UTC) for milestone 840 (the scheduler file is split along its seams):
the same lines, moved out of `kernel/src/sched.rs` into `kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs` unchanged, so the defect is
the same and only the path and line numbers differ.

